WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:05.440
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:12.545
&gt;&gt; You're seven years old sitting in
a classroom with 50 kids, you don't know.

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:19.585
The teacher is talking.

00:00:19.585 --> 00:00:30.488
[FOREIGN].

00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:33.330
You see her point at the blackboard.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:37.460
You hear her voice but
you do not understand a word.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:45.110
And you have just a few months to pass
an exam in this unknown language.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:49.350
That's no nightmare.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.586
That was my childhood growing up in India,

00:00:54.586 --> 00:00:59.964
moving every few years from a state
where everyone spoke Hindi,

00:00:59.964 --> 00:01:06.360
to a state where everyone spoke Bangla
to a state where everyone spoke Odia.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:15.471
At seven, when everything seemed
impossible, I wanted to cry,

00:01:15.471 --> 00:01:21.256
I wanted to run away,
I wanted to quit, but I tried my best.

00:01:24.785 --> 00:01:28.370
To push away my doubts and
fears and power through.

00:01:31.403 --> 00:01:35.270
At 9 when we moved again,
it got just a bit easier.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.384
Even though I could not talk
to my classmates for a while,

00:01:40.384 --> 00:01:43.006
we connected over cricket and volleyball.

00:01:43.006 --> 00:01:47.772
We shared delicious pranchas over lunch
and we teamed up at scouts after school.

00:01:50.363 --> 00:01:54.110
By 11, I was almost looking
forward to the next move.

00:01:56.083 --> 00:02:00.454
The promise of new friends,
new rivals, new foods,

00:02:00.454 --> 00:02:03.772
far outweighed the miscommunication.

00:02:08.847 --> 00:02:13.207
Living this experience as
a child was transformative.

00:02:13.207 --> 00:02:19.151
Over time, I got better and
better at shoving away my doubts and

00:02:19.151 --> 00:02:21.968
fears and powering through.

00:02:21.968 --> 00:02:23.520
Eventually the move stopped.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:31.050
But left behind, was this strange
tingling openness to try new things.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.060
You've heard the cliches,
take a leap of faith.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.130
Jump into the deep end and learn to swim.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.760
Fake it till you make it.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.840
Nobody told me any of that back then.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.850
For me it was simple.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:55.949
The discomfort goes away if you just
power through and power through I did.

00:02:55.949 --> 00:02:59.975
Before coming to the GSB,
I spent 15 years at a B2B tech company.

00:02:59.975 --> 00:03:06.806
Starting as an engineer in India and
pivoting over 10 times in those 15 years,

00:03:06.806 --> 00:03:11.570
across roles, functions,
teams, even geographies.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.352
I went out of my way to master new skills,

00:03:16.352 --> 00:03:21.730
working during the day and studying
evenings and weekends for three years.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:26.062
To get a business degree to pivot
to marketing, learning Spanish for

00:03:26.062 --> 00:03:28.390
presentations and deals in Mexico.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:30.844
Traveling the world,

00:03:30.844 --> 00:03:35.490
meeting customers to understand
their pain points always at work.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.660
I would settle for
nothing less than exceeding expectations.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:45.410
Powering through served me quite well,
until it didn't.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.326
When I got promoted to a manager,

00:03:49.326 --> 00:03:53.670
I put the same pressure on my team to
exceed expectations that I put on myself.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.462
I was sure they would get through
their discomfort by powering through.

00:03:59.462 --> 00:04:04.750
So when the first 360 reviews came in,
I was excited.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:08.998
I remember running upstairs this corner
conference room with frosted glass walls

00:04:08.998 --> 00:04:10.810
to read in private.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:13.670
And I opened my reviews one
by one the first one read.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:21.940
Such it should avoid being so pushy.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:25.100
It was as if those frosted glass
walls shattered around me.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:28.480
I could feel my energy go away.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.390
Who could be this malcontent?

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:37.513
&gt;&gt; With a little more hope I opened
the second review and it read,

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:45.383
Such it should trust and
empower the team more,

00:04:45.383 --> 00:04:48.950
and let go of the details, no.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:53.090
I could feel, the blood brain down
my head as I slouched into my chair.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:59.424
The reviews did not get
any better from there.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:05.606
By the time I got through them,
I felt like that seven year old again.

00:05:05.606 --> 00:05:10.518
I wanted to cry, I wanted to

00:05:10.518 --> 00:05:15.680
run away, I wanted to quit.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.410
And among those lines,
one line stayed with me.

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.540
Such it needs to pause and
smell a few more roses.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:25.695
Was I missing something?

00:05:25.695 --> 00:05:30.482
I realized not immediately, but
over the next several months, that all

00:05:30.482 --> 00:05:35.520
those doubts and fears I had shoved away
were still out there in the shadows.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.769
Powering through had a cost that
I hadn't reckoned with before.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.230
I had moved way too often to form
long term and deep friendships.

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:49.235
I felt this awkward distance with people
that others perceived as me being

00:05:49.235 --> 00:05:51.030
disconnected.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.559
The idea of home for
me was not tied to a particular place but

00:05:55.559 --> 00:05:58.065
only to a close group of people.

00:05:58.065 --> 00:06:01.945
Eventually, the 11 year
old in me took over.

00:06:01.945 --> 00:06:06.911
I powered through once again and I reached
out to a trusted mentor and coach for

00:06:06.911 --> 00:06:09.204
help, who convinced me to pause and

00:06:09.204 --> 00:06:12.120
reflect on the impact I
was having on my team.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.094
Gradually I learned to embrace the truth
that each member of my team had more years

00:06:19.094 --> 00:06:20.570
of experience than I did.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:26.160
I learned to align each member's
individual goals with that of the team and

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:28.330
the organization.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:32.330
I organized team building exercises
where there were no hierarchies.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.408
I lean them to listen
more to gain consensus,

00:06:35.408 --> 00:06:37.919
to understand their perspectives.

00:06:37.919 --> 00:06:42.735
But above everything I stopped
expecting them to power through every

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:44.570
discomfort the way I did.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:53.305
Standing here now, I know I talked to
an audience of people who powers through,

00:06:53.305 --> 00:06:59.040
who shoves away doubts and fears,
who takes leap after leap after leap.

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:06.406
And even though it works for you,
even though it works for us,

00:07:06.406 --> 00:07:11.243
we need to remember as we try to
change lives organizations and

00:07:11.243 --> 00:07:16.283
the world, that not all the people
we lead operate the same way.

00:07:18.483 --> 00:07:21.248
We need to have empathy and
understanding for

00:07:21.248 --> 00:07:25.810
the people on our teams who may
respond to discomfort differently.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:31.056
We cannot push them as
hard as we push ourselves.

00:07:31.056 --> 00:07:36.917
It is also important to know
that how we act sets an example,

00:07:36.917 --> 00:07:41.140
for our colleagues, for our employees and

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:45.964
as I recently realized,
even for our families.

00:07:45.964 --> 00:07:49.339
The other day my wife Meenal,and I
were discussing our next steps after

00:07:49.339 --> 00:07:50.020
graduation.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:53.890
We lived in Atlanta before
we got to Stanford.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:55.970
Should we go back or should we stay here?

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:59.420
So I turned to my almost
nine year old son.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:04.670
Ryan, what do you think Atlanta or
California?

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:11.365
Dad neither,
I think I want to try someplace new.

00:08:11.365 --> 00:08:18.300
At that moment I felt overwhelmed inside.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:24.062
So proud of his adventurous spirit
much like me when I was nine,

00:08:24.062 --> 00:08:30.060
yet concerned that he might also
miss a place that felt like home.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:38.920
There is a balance between always powering
through and pausing to smell the roses.

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:44.300
Between moving on and
enjoying where you are and what you have.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.155
I wish I could tell you exactly
where that balance lies, but

00:08:48.155 --> 00:08:50.440
I'm still trying to find it.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.200
So my ask of you today is this.

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:57.630
Keep in mind not only the benefits,
but also the costs of

00:08:57.630 --> 00:09:03.020
always powering through and
try to find a balance that works for you.

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:06.863
And just as importantly for
the people you lead.

00:09:06.863 --> 00:09:13.005
&gt;&gt; [MUSIC]

